---
import PlaygroundShell from '../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Simulate it" hint="Split a training budget with the approximation C = 6 x N x D, where N is the parameters and D the tokens, and D = ratio x N. Choose the budget and the tokens per parameter. The ratio of 20 comes from the Chinchilla results and is a rule of thumb, not a law.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Start with the defaults: <b>Compute budget</b> <b>1e23</b> FLOPs and <b>Tokens per parameter</b> <b>20</b>. The split is <b>28.9</b> billion parameters and <b>577.4</b> billion tokens, <b>100.0</b> percent of the parameters of the 20-token split.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Compute budget</b> to <b>5.88e23</b>: <b>70.0</b> billion parameters and <b>1400.0</b> billion tokens. That is the size and data of Chinchilla itself.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Compute budget</b> back to <b>1e23</b> and <b>Tokens per parameter</b> to <b>100</b>: the model shrinks to <b>12.9</b> billion parameters trained on <b>1291.0</b> billion tokens, only <b>44.7</b> percent as many parameters, so each generated token costs about that much less to serve.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Tokens per parameter</b> to <b>5</b>: <b>57.7</b> billion parameters on <b>288.7</b> billion tokens, <b>200.0</b> percent of the parameters. This is a big model on little data, the split that was too big for its data.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg scl" data-scl>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Compute budget <select class="pg-in" data-c><option value="1e21">1e21</option><option value="1e22">1e22</option><option value="1e23" selected>1e23</option><option value="5.88e23">5.88e23</option><option value="1e24">1e24</option><option value="1e25">1e25</option></select> FLOPs</label>
      <label>Tokens per parameter <input class="pg-in" type="range" min="5" max="200" step="5" value="20" data-r /> <b data-rv></b></label>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Parameters</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o1></span><span class="pg-note" data-n1></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b2><h3>Training tokens</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o2></span><span class="pg-note" data-n2></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Parameters compared with the 20-token split</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o3></span><span class="pg-note" data-n3></span></div>
    </div>
    <span class="pg-note" data-verdict></span>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query } from '../playground';

  // C = 6 N D and D = r N give N = sqrt(C / (6 r)). The 20-token split is the reference for the percentage.
  type Tone = 'good' | 'bad' | 'mid';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-scl]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const setBox = (i: number, value: string, note: string, tone: Tone) => {
      q(`[data-o${i}]`).textContent = value; q(`[data-n${i}]`).textContent = note;
      q<HTMLElement>(`[data-b${i}]`).style.setProperty('--c', tone === 'good' ? 'var(--good)' : tone === 'bad' ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--border)');
    };
    function draw() {
      const c = Number(q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-c]').value), r = Number(q<HTMLInputElement>('[data-r]').value);
      q('[data-rv]').textContent = String(r);
      const n = Math.sqrt(c / (6 * r)), d = r * n, n20 = Math.sqrt(c / 120), pct = (100 * n) / n20;
      setBox(1, `${(n / 1e9).toFixed(1)} billion`, `from N = sqrt(C / (6 x ${r}))`, 'mid');
      setBox(2, `${(d / 1e9).toFixed(1)} billion`, `${r} tokens for every parameter`, 'mid');
      setBox(3, `${pct.toFixed(1)} percent`, pct < 100 ? 'a smaller model: cheaper to serve, with more training tokens' : pct > 100 ? 'a bigger model on less data than the 20-token split' : 'the Chinchilla-style split', r === 20 ? 'good' : 'mid');
      q('[data-verdict]').textContent = r < 10 ? 'A big model on little data: the kind of split the Chinchilla study found was too heavy on size.' : r > 40 ? 'Far more data per parameter than the training-optimal split. This costs more to train for the same loss, and can pay off when the model is served a great deal.' : 'Close to the ratio that Chinchilla itself used. Treat it as a rule of thumb, not a law.';
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    root.addEventListener('change', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
